 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_XWwgtKhQDRhyY5m4",
   "name": "Pythagoras Theorem",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "327928691"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Pythagoras theorem"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Who is Pythagoras?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Pythagoras** was a **Greek mathematician** who lived over 2500 years ago\n- He is most famous for **Pythagoras’ theorem**, which includes the important formula for **right-angled triangles**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is Pythagoras' theorem?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Pythagoras' theorem **is a formula that links the **lengths** of the **three sides** of a **right-angled triangle**\n- The **longest side** of a right-angled triangle is called the **hypotenuse**\n\n  - The hypotenuse will always be the side **opposite **the right angle\n- **Pythagoras' theorem** states that  $a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}$\n\n  - $c$*  *is the **length **of the **hypotenuse**\n  - $a$  and  $b$  are the **lengths** of the **two shorter sides **\n  \n    - It does **not matter** which is labelled $a$ and which is labelled $b$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/42595-pythagoras-theorem-1.png",
   "file": "assets/d99d3bb5a83b-42595-pythagoras-theorem-1.png",
   "alt": "A right-angled triangle with the sides labelled a, b and c",
   "caption": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use Pythagoras’ theorem to find the length of the hypotenuse?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To find the length of the **hypotenuse**\n\n  - **Square** the lengths of the **two shorter sides**\n  - **Add** these two numbers together\n  - Take the positive **square root**\n- This can be written as $c=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}$\n\n  - This is just a **rearrangement** of the formula $a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}$ to make $c$** **the subject\n  - Note that when finding the hypotenuse you **add** inside the square root"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use Pythagoras’ theorem to find the length of a shorter side?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To find the length of a **shorter side**\n\n  - **Square** the lengths of the **hypotenuse** and the **other shorter side**\n  - **Subtract** these numbers to find the difference\n  - Take the positive **square root**\n- This can be written as $a=\\sqrt{c^{2}-b^{2}}$\n\n  - This is just a **rearrangement** of the formula $a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}$ to make $a$** **the subject\n  - Note that when finding one of the shorter sides you **subtract **inside the square root"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Can I use Pythagoras’ theorem with other shapes?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- You can use Pythagoras' theorem with any shape that can be **split into right-angled triangles**\n- You can find the **length **of the **diagonal **of a **rectangle** or **square**\n\n  - Use its diagonal to split the shape into two identical right-angled triangles\n  - The diagonal is now the hypotenuse of the right-angled triangles"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "If the hypotenuse ends up being shorter than another side in your answer then you have made a** mistake **somewhere.\n\nMake sure that you subtract the **smaller value from the bigger value** when finding the length of a shorter side.\n\nOtherwise you will get a \"**Math Error**\" when trying to find the square root of a negative number"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "In questions with **multiple steps**:\n\n- Leave your answer as an **exact answer**\n- **Do not round** until the very **end of the question**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "In the following diagram:\n$AB=12\\mathrm{cm}$\n$AC$ is a straight line, with $AD=9\\mathrm{cm}$ and $AC=22\\mathrm{cm}$\n\n![Back to Back Right Angled Triangles, IGCSE & GCSE Maths revision notes](assets/3ee6771c733c-15019-pythagoras-theorem-2.png)\n\nFind $x$, the length of side $BC.$ Give your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n> *To find *$x$*, we first need to find the length of *$BD$* using triangle *$ABD$\n*Note that *$BD$* is a shorter side*\n*Apply Pythagoras' theorem, *$a=\\sqrt{c^{2}-b^{2}}$\n\n$BD=\\sqrt{12^{2}-9^{2}}=\\sqrt{63}=7.93725...$\n\n> *It is best to leave rounding until the very end, use *$\\sqrt{63}$* (or *$3\\sqrt{7}$* if this is what your calculator has given you) in subsequent working*\n\n> *Find the length of *$DC$* by subtracting the length of *$AD$* from the length of *$AC$\n\n$DC=22-9=13\\mathrm{cm}$\n\n> *Now we can find *$x$* using triangle *$BCD$\n*Note that *$BC$* is the hypotenuse*\n*Apply Pythagoras' theorem, *$c=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}$\n\n`x equals square root of B D squared plus D C squared end root equals square root of open parentheses square root of 63 close parentheses squared plus 13 squared end root equals square root of 63 plus 169 end root`\n\n$x=\\sqrt{232}=15.23154621...$\n\n> *Round to 1 decimal place*\n\n$x=15.2\\mathrm{cm}$"
  }
